<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Genius Innovations – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> My Genius Innovations</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a speech on an academic topic. Interactive: Collaborate in developing plans for group activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a speech on innovations, providing examples to support ideas (e.g., "Businesses have started using renewable energy to cut costs and attract eco-conscious customers.") Can collaborate on planning a business idea to discuss potential outcomes (e.g., "If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers.") Can lead discussions to share plans for ideas and evaluate others' ideas (e.g., "I think launching an app could enhance customer engagement.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of recent successful innovations (e.g., self-driving cars, smart home devices, renewable energy solutions). Students work in pairs to quickly discuss which innovation they find most impactful and why, using vocabulary such as 'technology,' 'efficiency,' 'customer-focused,' and 'impactful.' The teacher encourages them to share initial ideas orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models a short, clear statement expressing an opinion about one innovation, incorporating target grammar (present perfect for innovations, first conditional) and professional language. For example: "Businesses have greatly enhanced customer engagement through social media apps. If we invest in robust marketing strategies, we might achieve similar success." The teacher also explicitly models the pronunciation of words containing the /ɔː/ sound (e.g., 'cost,' 'launch') and demonstrates the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like 'renewable' and 'successful,' prompting choral repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms related to business strategy and innovation.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'customer engagement' mean? Is it when customers buy once or when they feel connected to a brand?" (Expected answer: When they feel connected.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'If we develop a unique product, we might increase our customer base,' what does 'customer base' refer to? Is it the number of employees or the number of people who buy our product?" (Expected answer: The number of people who buy our product.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> Students listen to an audio recording of two young entrepreneurs discussing plans for a new sustainable business aimed at reducing waste. The dialogue naturally integrates the target vocabulary (e.g., 'innovation,' 'strategy,' 'efficiency,' 'costs,' 'app,' 'collaboration,' 'entrepreneur,' 'target,' 'launch') and grammatical structures (first conditional, present perfect for innovations, future continuous) as they brainstorm and evaluate ideas. The focus is on the interactive aspect of planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio clip of a business pitch discussion (authentic-like dialogue), approximately 2-3 minutes long, designed for a B1.2 level. The register is professional yet conversational.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participating actively in conversations to achieve a shared goal and expressing opinions, ideas, and suggestions in an organized manner during discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main innovation the entrepreneurs are discussing?" (Students briefly share their answer with a partner.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and decide if statements about their strategies are True or False (e.g., "They have decided to launch an app to increase visibility."). Students justify their answers orally, discussing what they heard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Speaking focus):</b> Students match proposed solutions to potential problems mentioned in the audio (e.g., match "develop a customer-focused app" with "enhance customer engagement"). They then orally explain their matches to a partner using phrases like, "I think they should implement X because it will improve Y."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking fluency and accuracy in using First conditional, Present perfect for innovations, and Future continuous. Target vocabulary: 'innovation,' 'business,' 'strategy,' 'marketing,' 'customer base,' 'automation,' 'efficiency,' 'launch,' 'collaborate,' 'promote,' 'enhance,' 'invest,' 'engage.' Pragmatic functions: Asking about business strategies ("What strategies have increased customer engagement?"), Sharing ideas for innovation ("If we use social media, we can reach a larger audience."). Sociolinguistic: Using professional language and showing respect.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion & Oral Repetition:</b> Students receive sentences with blanks related to business innovations. They complete the blanks with appropriate vocabulary words (e.g., 'strategy,' 'launch,' 'promote,' 'engage') and then read the full sentences aloud in pairs, focusing on correct pronunciation of /ɔː/ and multisyllabic words.
                        <br>Example: "Local shops have begun using social media to ______ their products." (promote)
                        <br>"If we ______ an app, we might ______ customer engagement." (launch, enhance)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Business Strategy Questions:</b> In pairs, students use provided prompts (e.g., "increase visibility / host promotional events," "reduce costs / automate processes," "attract customer base / develop unique product") to form questions and answers using the first conditional and present perfect.
                        <br>Student A: "What strategies have businesses implemented to increase visibility?"
                        <br>Student B: "Many businesses have hosted promotional events. If we host a similar event, we will definitely promote our brand."
                        <br>Student A: "How can we reduce costs?"
                        <br>Student B: "If we automate our supply chain, we'll be streamlining processes, which will reduce costs significantly."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Hypothetical Innovation Discussions:</b> Students interview each other about hypothetical business challenges, practicing asking about strategies and sharing innovative ideas. They use questions like, "What innovative strategy have you developed to improve customer satisfaction?" and respond with first conditional statements like, "If we streamline our feedback process, we might enhance customer engagement." They ensure to use professional language and show respect for each other's suggestions.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Business Innovation Pitch. Students work in groups of three to develop a "genius innovation" (a new product or service) and prepare a short (2-3 minute) pitch for it. They must collaborate to plan their business idea and present their strategy, potential outcomes, and future steps.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The task directly relates to "My Genius Innovations" and "Entrepreneurial Spirit," requiring students to think creatively about a business and how to market it. Each group must identify a problem, propose an innovative solution, and explain its business strategy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Formation and Brainstorming (10 min):</b> Students form groups of three. Each group brainstorms an innovative product or service. They must discuss its core function, target customer base, and initial ideas for marketing and implementation. They are encouraged to use the target vocabulary and pragmatic functions during their discussion (e.g., "I think we should create an app to increase visibility," "If we automate the production, we might reduce costs").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Planning the Pitch (10 min):</b> Groups plan their short pitch, outlining the problem their innovation solves, their unique solution, their business model, and their strategic approach. They must include at least two sentences using the first conditional, two using the present perfect for innovations, and one using the future continuous. They also consider professional language for their presentation (e.g., "This strategy could attract more customers to our project.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pitch Presentation (5 min per group):</b> Each group presents their "genius innovation" to the class (or another group), explaining their idea and strategies. Other groups are encouraged to ask respectful questions and evaluate ideas using professional language (e.g., "Your idea adds an interesting approach. What strategies have you considered for customer engagement?"). The teacher circulates, offering support and noting fluency and communication effectiveness.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">25-30 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking criteria and peer assessment for collaborative interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's "Genius Innovation Pitch" and the subsequent Q&A session serve as the assessment task. The teacher observes individual and group contributions to collaboration and presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, including long vowel /ɔː/ and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'innovation,' 'strategy,' 'customer base,' 'launch,' 'enhance').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of First conditional (e.g., "If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of Present perfect for innovations (e.g., "Local shops have begun using social media...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of Future continuous (e.g., "I'll be analyzing the market trends.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas and logical flow in the pitch.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group and during Q&A.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of professional language and showing respect for others' ideas.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral reflection or quick written note):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your group's innovation pitch do you think was most impactful, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did your group collaborate to develop and present your idea?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary or grammar structures did you find most useful today when sharing your innovation ideas?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you would improve about your speaking or collaboration in a future group presentation?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., most students actively engaged in collaboration, clear use of first conditional).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more targeted practice on future continuous, strategies for encouraging quieter students to speak up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did students manage the B1.2 level speaking demands, particularly the pronunciation of /ɔː/ and multisyllabic words?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), we will take these innovative ideas and business strategies we discussed today and transform them into a formal written proposal, focusing on structuring our arguments and using clear, persuasive language, expanding on the vocabulary and grammar we practiced for speaking."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>